  "narrated": {
   "duration": 106.8,
   "narrated": true,
   "transcript": [
    "Online debate tells you who is loudest. It rarely tells you what most people think.",
    "Society Speaks starts with a statement and three buttons.",
    "You can agree, disagree, or say you are unsure. Each vote takes a second, and you do not need an account.",
    "As the votes come in, people are grouped by how they vote, not by who they are. Then it shows you three things.",
    "First, where people agree. Second, the ideas that win support in every group. And third, where the real divisions lie.",
    "Every figure carries its margin of error, and the code is open for anyone to check.",
    "For readers, there is a question to answer each day, and a free daily brief that shows how left, centre and right are covering the news.",
    "And if you want more, a paid personal brief turns the sources you choose into one email each morning.",
    "For publishers, the same voting sits under an article, in place of the comment section.",
    "Councils and public bodies can run a full consultation, across several questions at once.",
    "And now any organisation can run one itself, with a 14-day free trial. Ask one question, then share a link or put a QR code on the big screen. The report shows where your audience agrees, disagrees, is unsure or is split.",
    "There is even a daily game, where you govern and every choice costs something.",
    "Society Speaks. Making disagreement useful again."
   ],
